### **<span class="mark">Am I eligible for gastric sleeve surgery?</span>**

<span class="mark">A sleeve gastrectomy is generally considered suitable
for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30–35 with associated
weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood
pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.</span>

<span class="mark">Calculate your BMI.  
  
Your BMI is a great starting point, however your surgeon will take the
time to assess your whole health picture to determine if you are a good
candidate for a gastric sleeve.</span>

**<span class="mark">You may also be a candidate for a gastric sleeve if
you:</span>**

- <span class="mark">have tried to lose weight previously through diet
  and lifestyle changes without lasting success</span>

- <span class="mark">are open to making long-term dietary changes and
  committing to ongoing aftercare including post-op check ins and
  dietitian follow-ups</span>

- <span class="mark">are not experiencing any untreated health
  conditions that would make surgery unsafe (some conditions may need
  management first rather than ruling surgery out altogether)</span>

<span class="mark">While it can be life-changing, weight loss surgery is
a tool, not a one-off fix. The best way to explore your options is to
schedule an initial consultation with a</span> <span class="mark">Perth
bariatric surgeon.</span>

### <span class="mark"> **Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth: how it works** </span>

1.  <span class="mark">**Initial consultation and assessment**  
    Here you’ll meet your surgeon, talk through your medical history and
    weight loss goals, ask as many questions as you like, and receive a
    professional opinion about whether a gastric sleeve is suitable for
    you and your lifestyle.  
    </span>

2.  <span class="mark">**Pre-surgery preparation**  
    You may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver
    and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). You won’t do this
    alone!</span> <span class="mark">Obesity Surgery WA’s dietitians and
    nursing staff are on-hand to support you throughout.  
    </span>

3.  <span class="mark">**Procedure day  **
    A sleeve gastrectomy requires a general anaesthesia and is performed
    via keyhole surgery. Total surgical time is ~45–60 minutes. Your
    surgeon removes ~75–80% of your stomach and seals the new ‘sleeve’
    with a staple line.  
    </span>

4.  <span class="mark">**Hospital recovery**  
    You’ll spend 1-2 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private
    Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park
    Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki
    Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
    Guided and monitored by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll be on
    fluids to start before being cleared to head home.  
    </span>

5.  <span class="mark">**Returning to work and daily life**  
    Most light daily activities can usually be resumed gradually within
    the first fortnight after a gastric sleeve. You’ll be prescribed ~2
    weeks’ rest from desk work, however physical or manual labour will
    require closer to 4–6 weeks.  
    </span>

6.  <span class="mark">**Ongoing support**  
    Progression from fluids only → thicker liquids and purees → regular
    textures will take place with the support of your</span>
    <span class="mark">dietitian. Plus you’ll need ongoing reviews
    (regularly post-op then annually) and bloodwork done to protect your
    nutrition and results long-term.</span>

### <span class="mark">**Benefits of gastric sleeve surgery**  Beyond weight loss alone, gastric sleeve surgery offers a range of benefits that can improve both your physical health and day-to-day quality of life. </span>

1.  <span class="mark">Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated
    clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months,
    though individual results vary.</span>

2.  <span class="mark">Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach
    size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller
    portions feel more satisfying.</span>

3.  <span class="mark">Improvement in weight-related conditions such as
    type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, and joint pain,
    though outcomes will differ between individuals.</span>

4.  <span class="mark">No intestinal rerouting; food travels through the
    digestive tract in its normal order, meaning no malabsorption as is
    seen with a bypass.</span>

5.  <span class="mark">Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally
    less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery
    time) than open surgery.</span>

6.  <span class="mark">Single permanent procedure; no implanted device
    to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.  
    </span>

### **<span class="mark">Risks of gastric sleeve surgery</span>**

### <span class="mark">Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail. </span>

1.  <span class="mark">Staple line leak; though it is a serious
    potential complication and may require further treatment, it only
    occurs in ~1–3% of cases.</span>

2.  <span class="mark">Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if
    pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles,
    though for others it can be persistent.</span>

3.  <span class="mark">Nutritional deficiencies (particularly vitamin
    B12, iron, and vitamin D) are characteristic of the reduced stomach
    size, meaning vitamin supplements and regular blood tests are
    recommended long-term for your health.</span>

4.  <span class="mark">Hair thinning in the months after surgery is
    typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional
    intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake
    stabilises.</span>

5.  <span class="mark">Weight regain is also possible if your eating
    habits drift back to pre-operative ‘normal’ and/or your follow-up
    care is interrupted or lapses; the success of a gastric sleeve works
    best alongside continued care and support.</span>

6.  <span class="mark">General surgical risks include bleeding,
    infection, blood clots, and anaesthetic reactions, as with any
    operation.</span>

<span class="mark">**How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?  **
  
Gastric sleeve surgery cost varies depending on your level of private
health insurance cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital and
theatre charges, and any out-of-pocket gap that applies to your
situation. [<u>Medicare item number
31575</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?type=item&q=31575&qt=ItemID)
does attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private
health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the
hospital and theatre component. Your surgeon’s fee for a primary
laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is \$4,000 for both insured and
uninsured patients, rising to \$5,000 for a complex case. For those
without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a
concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain
circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain
psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing
options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients. A
full breakdown of costs, funding pathways, and pre-surgical clinical
fees</span> <span class="mark">can be found here.</span>

### **<span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>**

<span class="mark">Ready to explore whether a gastric sleeve could
change things for you? Let's talk.  
Alt  
Ready to find out if a gastric sleeve is right for you? Book a
consultation today.</span>

### **<span class="mark">FAQs (8 questions)</span>**

<span class="mark">**1. Is the gastric sleeve permanent, and can it be
reversed?  **
Yes, a gastric sleeve is permanent; a portion of your stomach is removed
and cannot be reattached. However, if further intervention is needed
down the track, a sleeve *can* be converted to a gastric bypass or
another procedure. Your surgeon will discuss long-term expectations with
you during your consultation.</span>

<span class="mark">**2. How much weight will I actually lose?  **
On average, gastric sleeve patients lose around 60–70% of their excess
body weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary. Factors
like starting weight, age, diet, and lifestyle will influence outcomes.
Some weight regain over time is not uncommon and long-term success is
generally supported by ongoing follow-up with your surgical and dietetic
team.</span>

<span class="mark">**3. Will I have to take vitamins for the rest of my
life?  **
Yes, a lifelong commitment to vitamins and supplements is strongly
recommended. Although the sleeve doesn't reroute digestion, eating
significantly less makes it harder to meet nutritional needs through
food alone. Iron, B12, vitamin D, and calcium are among the most
commonly monitored nutrients. Your dietitian will advise you on
supplements, and regular blood tests help keep levels on track.</span>

<span class="mark">**4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric
bypass?  **
A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway
unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the
small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass
may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with
significant reflux better.</span>

<span class="mark">**5. What does recovery actually look like week to
week?  **
Your first 1-2 days post op are spent in hospital on clear fluids. Week
one is rest at home, sipping carefully and managing fatigue. By weeks
two to three, your diet progresses from fluids to pureed and soft foods.
Light daily activity and desk-style work can resume within a fortnight,
whereas more physical activity and manual work should wait for four to
six weeks.</span>

<span class="mark">**6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right
for me?  **
Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon,
as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those
with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable
option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.</span>

<span class="mark">**7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?  **
[<u>Medicare item number
31575</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?type=item&q=31575&qt=ItemID)
attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of
the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost
and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health
insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the
hospital component. A</span> <span class="mark">full fee breakdown is
available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.</span>

<span class="mark">**8. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?  **
A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but
having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your
specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the
appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has your relevant medical
history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can
advise you on next steps.</span>

