   274	<span class="mark">**3. Will I have to take vitamins for the rest of my
   275	life?  **
   276	Yes, a lifelong commitment to vitamins and supplements is strongly
   277	recommended. Although the sleeve doesn't reroute digestion, eating
   278	significantly less makes it harder to meet nutritional needs through
   279	food alone. Iron, B12, vitamin D, and calcium are among the most
   280	commonly monitored nutrients. Your dietitian will advise you on
   281	supplements, and regular blood tests help keep levels on track.</span>
   282	
   283	<span class="mark">**4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric
   284	bypass?  **
   285	A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway
   286	unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the
   287	small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass
   288	may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with
   289	significant reflux better.</span>
   290	
   291	<span class="mark">**5. What does recovery actually look like week to
   292	week?  **
   293	Your first 1-2 days post op are spent in hospital on clear fluids. Week
   294	one is rest at home, sipping carefully and managing fatigue. By weeks
   295	two to three, your diet progresses from fluids to pureed and soft foods.
   296	Light daily activity and desk-style work can resume within a fortnight,
   297	whereas more physical activity and manual work should wait for four to
   298	six weeks.</span>
   299	
   300	<span class="mark">**6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right
   301	for me?  **
   302	Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon,
   303	as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those
   304	with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable
   305	option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.</span>
   306	
   307	<span class="mark">**7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?  **
   308	[<u>Medicare item number
   309	31575</u>](https://www9.health.gov.au/mbs/fullDisplay.cfm?type=item&q=31575&qt=ItemID)
   310	attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of
   311	the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost
   312	and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health
   313	insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the
   314	hospital component. A</span> <span class="mark">full fee breakdown is
   315	available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.</span>
   316	
   317	<span class="mark">**8. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?  **
   318	A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but
   319	having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your
   320	specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the
   321	appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has your relevant medical
   322	history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can
   323	advise you on next steps.</span>
